A non-resistant monster still gets a chance of resisting anyway.  zap.c,
line 4073:

  resisted = rn2(100 + alev - dlev) < mtmp->data->mr;

For a wand, alev = 12.  For the Wizard, dlev = 30 (or whatever you've
drained him to), and mtmp->data->mr = 100.  So if you drain him down to
lvl10, you'll have a 2 in 102 chance of the wand being successful.
(Multiply up for bouncing the ray.)

A more reliable way to send him to sleep would be the AD_SLEE attack.
But:

 Exactly four types of monster can do that in vanilla, and you can only
 polyself into two of those.

 If it's a breath attack, then it still comes back to that resist()
 function, so he still gets a very high saving throw.

 The one remaining monster (homunculus) doesn't have a very good chance
 of hitting the Wizard, with his AC of -8.
 
This might be an opportunity for barehanded Slash'EM drows, though.

